Therapist: Hi Elena, thanks for coming in today. How have you been since our last session?
Client: Honestly, it's been a really hard couple of weeks. Losing the job at the warehouse last month hit harder than I expected, and then Daniel and I broke up on top of it.
Therapist: That's a lot to be carrying at once. Can you tell me more about how the breakup happened?
Client: We'd been having problems for a while, but he ended it over text, which felt so cold after four years. I moved back to my apartment on Birchwood Lane — the one at 1420, apartment 3B — after staying with him for the last year and a half.
Therapist: That's a significant amount of change happening all at once — the relationship, your living situation, and your job, all within a few weeks of each other.
Client: Yeah. Some days I handle it fine, and other days I can't get off the couch.
Therapist: When you say you can't get off the couch, what does that actually look like for you?
Client: Just laying there for hours. Not really eating much. I called in sick to the temp job I picked up because I couldn't make myself get dressed.
Therapist: How many days this week did that happen?
Client: Three, maybe four.
Therapist: Let's talk about the job search side of things for a moment too, since that's a piece we haven't checked in on yet today. How has that been going?
Client: Slow. I've applied to maybe ten places since the warehouse let me go, and I've heard back from exactly one, and that was a rejection.
Therapist: How has that response rate been affecting how you feel about the search overall?
Client: It makes it feel pointless some days, like why bother sending out another application if nothing's coming back anyway.
Therapist: That's an understandable reaction to a slow, discouraging process, especially layered on top of everything else you're managing right now.
Client: Yeah. It's just a lot all landing at once.
Therapist: Before we go further, how has the box breathing we practiced a few sessions ago been working, if you've been using it at all?
Client: I used it a couple times, actually. It helped in the moment, but it doesn't really touch the nighttime stuff — that's more just this heavy, tired feeling than actual panic.
Therapist: That's a useful distinction — the breathing is well-suited for acute anxious activation, but a heavy, low mood at night is a different kind of experience that probably needs a different kind of tool. We'll come back to that. I want to check in on something important, and I ask this with everyone going through what you're going through, not because of anything specific you've said — has any of this made you think about hurting yourself, or that you'd be better off not being here?
Client: I mean... there were a couple of nights last week where I thought everyone would probably be better off without me around. But I didn't do anything. I don't have a plan or anything like that.
Therapist: Thank you for telling me that honestly — I know that's not easy to say out loud. I want to ask a few more questions so I understand the full picture, is that okay?
Client: Yeah, that's fine.
Therapist: When those thoughts came up, did you think about a specific way you might act on them?
Client: No, nothing specific. It was more just this passive "what's the point" feeling, mostly late at night when I couldn't sleep.
Therapist: Have you ever attempted to harm yourself in the past, at any point in your life?
Client: No, never. I've had low periods before but nothing like actually trying anything.
Therapist: Do you have access to anything at home right now that worries you, when you're in that state of mind?
Client: No, nothing like that. It's really more just the exhaustion talking.
Therapist: That's important to know. What's kept you going on the nights you've had that thought?
Client: My sister, honestly. Camila calls me every night around nine just to check in, and I don't want to worry her. And my cat needs someone to feed her.
Therapist: Those both sound like real anchors for you right now. Given what you've shared, I'd like us to build a short safety plan together today — not because I think you're in immediate danger, but because it gives us both something concrete for the nights that get hard. Would that be okay?
Client: Yeah, I think that would actually help. I don't like feeling like I don't have a plan for what to do.
Therapist: Let's start with warning signs — what tells you a bad night is starting, before the thoughts even show up?
Client: Usually it's when I start skipping meals, or when I don't answer texts for hours. That's usually the first sign.
Therapist: Good, those are concrete and noticeable. Next — when you notice those signs, what's one thing you can do on your own to cope, even something small?
Client: Putting on a show I've already seen helps, something familiar. And petting my cat actually calms me down more than people expect.
Therapist: Those both count. Have there been other things that have helped you get through hard nights in the past, even before all of this started?
Client: Taking a hot shower, sometimes. And I used to journal a lot in college, though I haven't done that in years.
Therapist: Would you be open to bringing journaling back in, even just a few lines on the hard nights?
Client: Maybe. It always helped me get the spiraling thoughts out of my head and onto paper instead.
Therapist: That's worth trying again, especially alongside the shower and the show. Now, who's someone you can reach out to if those coping strategies aren't enough?
Client: Camila, for sure. She's told me to call anytime, even at 2 a.m.
Therapist: I'd like to actually practice that call for a moment, if you're open to it — not because I doubt you'd do it, but because rehearsing it now can make it easier to actually pick up the phone in the moment, when everything feels harder to do. What would you actually say to her, if you called at, say, eleven at night because things felt heavy?
Client: Probably something like, "Hey, I'm having a rough night, can you just stay on the phone with me for a bit?"
Therapist: That's clear and direct, and it tells her exactly what you need without you having to over-explain in a moment when talking might feel hard. How do you imagine she'd respond?
Client: She'd definitely stay on. She's told me that a hundred times, I just don't always believe it in the moment.
Therapist: That gap between what you know logically and what you believe in a hard moment is really common, and it's part of why having the script ready ahead of time helps — you don't have to trust your judgment in the moment, you just have to run the plan. I'll write her down as your primary support contact. And if it ever got to the point where you were worried about your immediate safety, in a moment where none of this feels like enough — what's the plan then?
Client: I guess call 988, or go to the ER if it's really bad.
Therapist: Exactly right, and I'm glad you already knew that number. I'm also going to make a referral to Dr. Okonkwo-Reyes, the psychiatrist who works with our practice, so you can talk through whether medication might help support you through this period, alongside our sessions. Would you be open to that?
Client: Yeah, I think I'm ready to actually consider it this time. I've said no before, but I don't want to keep white-knuckling through this alone.
Therapist: I'll get that referral sent today. Given what you've shared, I'd also like to see you again sooner than our usual weekly slot — how does this Thursday work, just three days out, instead of waiting a full week?
Client: Thursday works. I don't have anything going on since I'm still between jobs.
Therapist: Good. Before we wrap up, I want to spend a few minutes on something that might help with the couch days specifically — a technique called behavioral activation, which is really just deliberately scheduling one small activity a day, even when you don't feel like it.
Client: Okay, what would that look like for me?
Therapist: Something low effort but not nothing — a ten-minute walk, texting a friend back, doing one dish. The goal isn't motivation first, it's action first, and motivation tends to follow afterward more than people expect.
Client: That actually makes sense. I always wait to feel like doing something, and then I never do.
Therapist: Right, so for this week, I want you to pick one small thing to do each day, and text it to yourself or write it down so we can look at the pattern together on Thursday.
Client: I can do that. I'll probably start with just getting the mail every day, honestly, since I've been letting it pile up.
Therapist: That's a perfect example — small, concrete, and doable even on a hard day. What about the job applications — would it feel realistic to fold one small application-related task into that same daily list, or does that feel like too much on top of everything else right now?
Client: Maybe just one a day instead of trying to do five in a row like I was before and burning out on it.
Therapist: That's a much more sustainable pace, and it keeps the search moving without setting up another all-or-nothing cycle. One more thing before we close — I want to make sure Camila knows a little about what's going on, at whatever level of detail you're comfortable with, so she's not just checking in blind. Would you be willing to tell her about today's conversation, even briefly?
Client: I think I can tell her I brought up the "not wanting to be here" thoughts with you, and that we made a plan together. I don't think I need to give her every detail.
Therapist: That sounds like a good level of disclosure — enough that she understands the plan and can support it, without you having to relive everything with her too. To summarize where we landed today: we built a safety plan together, including a script for calling Camila on hard nights, I'm sending the referral to Dr. Okonkwo-Reyes for a medication consultation, we're meeting again this Thursday instead of next week, and you're going to try one small behavioral activation task plus one small job-search task each day between now and then.
Client: Okay. Thank you for not freaking out on me when I told you that. It made it easier to just say it.
Therapist: I'm really glad you told me. That took courage, and it's going to help us take better care of you. I'll see you Thursday, Elena.
Client: Thanks. See you Thursday.
